  )

  (text "USB-C connector subsystem (pilink-7el.7). Device-only (UFP) wiring, rear-exit\nplacement per the user's requirement that the cable run straight back out of the\nenclosure. USB 2.0 only -- RP2350B has no USB3 PHY -- so no CC logic/PD chip is\nneeded, just passive 5.1k pulldowns on CC1/CC2 (Rd, advertising \"sink, default\nUSB power\" per the USB-C spec).\n\nJ401: LCSC C165948 (TYPE-C-31-M-12, mfr Hroparts/HRO), a generic 16-pin SMD\nUSB-C receptacle, 514,015 units in stock at research time, Extended-library part\n(standard extended-part handling fee, not a sourcing-availability risk). Pinout\nverified directly from the datasheet's own table (image-only PDF, rendered and\nread, not guessed) and cross-checked pin-for-pin against KiCad 8.0.9's stock\nConnector:USB_C_Receptacle_USB2.0_16P symbol -- an exact match, including the\nA1/A12/B1/B12 GND and A4/A9/B4/B9 VBUS pins, which the stock symbol already\nstacks onto one visible pin each (hidden duplicate pin numbers at the same\ngraphical point), so a single wire to that point ties all 4 physical pins\ntogether internally -- no manual tying needed for GND/VBUS, only for the two\nD+/D- pairs below. Footprint: KiCad's stock\nConnector_USB:USB_C_Receptacle_HRO_TYPE-C-31-M-12 -- a purpose-built footprint\nfor this exact manufacturer part number, not a generic stand-in.\n\nDP1/DP2 (A6/B6, both datasheet-labeled D+) are tied together and routed to the\nUSB_DP global label; DN1/DN2 (A7/B7, both D-) are tied together and routed to\nUSB_DM. Both global labels are reused exactly as already declared in\nrp2350b_memory.kicad_sch (confirmed via grep before use, same case/text) --\nRP2350B's own USB PHY handles the shorted-together pairs fine at USB2 speeds;\nthis is the standard low-cost pattern for a connector that exposes both plug\norientations' D+/D- separately, not a hack. SBU1/SBU2 (A8/B8) are left\nunconnected (no_connect flags) -- no DisplayPort/Thunderbolt alt-mode in this\ndesign. The SHIELD pin (S1, the connector's metal shell/mounting-tab contacts --\nnot part of the USB-C spec's 16 signal/power pins, and not mentioned in the\noriginal pin-table investigation) is tied directly to GND, the standard\nchassis-to-signal-ground tie used by most USB-C reference designs for a\nsingle-ground-plane board like this one.\n" (at 20 20 0)
    (effects (font (size 1.27 1.27)) (justify left top))
    (uuid a00ebf83-9056-4b8e-9e59-0b66b52db85d)
  )

  (text "F401: VBUS overcurrent protection, sized properly per pilink-imx after the\nfirst attempt (C269104, 500mA hold) was removed on review for being\nundersized. Chosen: LCSC C269106 (Brightking SMD0805B100TFT), an 0805 PTC\nresettable fuse, 1A hold / 1.95A trip / 6V max / R@25C=40mOhm, 5,460 units in\nstock at research time -- lower than its siblings but not zero, worth\nrechecking at order time. In series between the connector's VBUS and the +5V\nnet power.kicad_sch expects from this sheet, same position the earlier,\nremoved attempt used.\n\nSizing: worst-case steady current re-derived from power.kicad_sch's own\nbudget (RP2350B tens of mA + ESP32-WROOM-32U up to 500mA per Espressif's\nTX-burst spec + display ~50-100mA incl. backlight) = ~0.6-0.65A. Picked the\n1A hold variant (C269106) over the 750mA sibling (C269105, R@25C=90mOhm)\nbecause 0.65A is 65% of a 1A hold rating vs 87% of a 750mA rating -- 65%\nleaves headroom for the ambient-temperature hold-current derating all PTC\nfuses exhibit once enclosed (this family's own derating curve is a scanned,\nnon-text-extractable image in its datasheet, so this uses the general\n<=75-80%-of-rated guideline for this part class as a flagged assumption, not\nread off this datasheet's own curve). R@25C=40mOhm adds only ~26mV drop at\nthe 0.65A worst case, negligible against the AMS1117's own dropout margin\n(already flagged elsewhere on this sheet/power.kicad_sch as tight against\nUSB's worst-case 4.5V VBUS floor) -- this fuse doesn't meaningfully worsen\nthat pre-existing, already-documented concern.\n\nR401/R402: 5.1k ohm Rd pulldowns on CC1/CC2 to GND, Resistor_SMD:R_0402_1005Metric\n(same footprint convention as the other pull resistors in this project, e.g.\ninput.kicad_sch's A/B/X/Y pull-ups). 5.1k is the USB-C spec's standard sink-side\nRd value, not a project-specific choice.\n\nReferences use a new 400-499 block for this sheet (power.kicad_sch: 1-99,\nrp2350b_memory.kicad_sch: 100-199, esp32_bt.kicad_sch: 200-299,\ninput.kicad_sch: 300-399) -- J401, F401, R401-R402, #PWR401-#PWR405. The\nstill-empty display.kicad_sch is expected to take 500-599 whenever it's\npopulated, though that bead owns the final call.\n\nNo PWR_FLAG is added on this sheet's +5V or GND nets: power.kicad_sch already\ncarries both (#FLG01 on +5V, #FLG02 on GND), satisfying ERC project-wide for\nthose two nets regardless of what drives them downstream. The USB-C connector's\nown VBUS/GND pins are \"passive\"-type pins (not \"power_out\"), so wiring them\ninto +5V/GND here does not create a second driver or an ERC \"conflicting power\noutputs\" error -- same reasoning already documented on esp32_bt.kicad_sch for\nwhy a local PWR_FLAG should NOT be added once a real upstream driver/flag\nexists." (at 20 170 0)
    (effects (font (size 1.27 1.27)) (justify left top))
    (uuid 81b2d06f-5ed5-4a3b-b481-333ac5f3ba51)
  )

)
